 ### [de Casteljau-based root finding](1596-de-casteljau-based-root-finding.md)
-An algorithm to find the intersection of a control polygon with the zero axis, implemented with constant memory usage and without index operations [S2]. It uses the de Casteljau algorithm to subdivide the control polygon [S2]. It exhibits quadratic convergence speed and quadratic complexity for each step [S2]. It is suggested as the best overall root-finder among variants due to its balance of performance and stability, especially for volume rendering where a fixed number of 3 to 7 iterations is often used [S2].
+A root finding algorithm for polynomials in Bernstein form that isolates and refines the first intersection of a viewing ray with an isosurface by subdividing the control polygon using the de Casteljau algorithm at its intersection with the zero axis. It sets itself apart from knot insertion methods like [MR07] by offering quadratic convergence speed and constant memory usage without index operations, making it significantly faster for GPU architectures.
 
 ### Sturm Series root finding
-A root finding approach based on Sturm Series working in power form [S2]. It is a root-finder in power form using the Sturm Series to count multiplicities of roots in a given interval [S2]. It uses binary subdivision of the search interval [S2]. The algorithm computes the Sturm Series at the beginning, evaluates it once during every iteration step, and subdivides the search interval in a binary manner [S2]. Numerical problems natural for power form implementations get more severe for more iterations [S2]. It exhibits exponential convergence with constant memory consumption during iteration, but high memory consumption overall as the whole Sturm Series needs to be kept in memory during the entire execution [S2]. For degree 9 (tri-cubic), execution time is 0.077 (relative), MSE is 0.003, and max err is 1.0 [S2].
