 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Roughly a factor of three faster than Schwarze's method [S1, unofficial]. The cost is dominated by solving the quadratic equation for extrema and evaluating the polynomial [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Low [S1, unofficial]. Requires storing only a few floating-point variables for the interval and extrema [S1, unofficial]. The Sturm Series variant has high memory consumption as it keeps the whole series in memory during the entire execution [S2].
+- **Time Complexity:** Roughly a factor of three faster than Schwarze code while providing same guarantees on correctness and better numerical stability [S1, unofficial]. The cost is dominated by solving the quadratic equation for extrema and evaluating the polynomial [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Low for the isolation method [S1, unofficial]. High for Sturm Series variant as the whole series is kept in memory [S2].
 - **Measured Performance:** For degree 9 (tri-cubic) polynomials, the method (referred to as "RF" or Regula Falsi in comparisons) shows relative execution time of 0.062-0.077 compared to Schwarze's 1.0, with low Mean Squared Error (0.003-0.016) and max error (0.66-1.0) [S2].
 - **Strengths:** Correctness guarantees similar to Schwarze's method; better numerical stability than Neubauer's; well-suited for SIMD parallel implementation due to little branching and regular control flow [S1, unofficial].
